A||||4||ezsurf||Fan Temp Switch||||1||
Z||000000||ezsurf||04-12-2000||10:28 PM||||Rodney Dickman has a fan temp switch that activates the radiator fan at 200F and turns it off at 185F.  His switch sells for $30.00.  A friend of mine told me it looks like a switch out of a Ford Mustang.  I suspect it is a stock part out of another car.  It probably retails for about $10.00 or less.  I'd like to know if anyone has this switch.  If so, what is the part number?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Frank R||04-13-2000||03:17 AM||||Napa can tell you all the temp sensors that will fit a GM car with a particular temp fan turn on.  BUT, the fan sensor will never open to stop the fan, because the thermostat is going to keep the temps at whatever the rating of the thermostat is.  The temp sensor will try to bring temps down to the point the fan could stop operating.  NO way.  Of course this is only my experience.  Frank R in Idaho||||reg||6
Z||000002||mwbackus||04-13-2000||10:14 AM||||The lower temp fan switch is used in conjunction with a lower temp thermostat:<P>180° Thermostat (Stant Part #35818)  $8.50<P>Note : Price quoted is from Checker Auto Parts.<P>The Hypertech equivalent (Part #4028) for this 180° fan switch retails for just over $40.<P>Mike B.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||ezsurf||04-13-2000||11:31 AM||||I hear you Frank.  The loweer temp fan switch needs to be used in conjunction with the 180F thermostat.  I have the 180F thermostat.  However, I'm sure there are many others who have the same problem I do.  For every part I fix, I find two more broken.  Repair costs continue to sky rocket with no end in sight.  The average fan switch costs $5 to $10.  I'm sure no one enjoys paying 3 to 5 times that amount.  I'm sure no one made a special production run of these switches.  If they had, they would cost even more.  It would be advantageous to simply find out what car the switch was made for.  Then we could all enjoy a quick easy fix.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-13-2000||12:23 PM||||The job of the thermostat is to control MINIMUM coolant temperature. The thermostat DOES NOT limit maximum coolant temperature. Maximum coolant temperature is limited by the rest of the cooling system of which the fan switch is a big factor.<P>The stock fan switch is 235F for the single speed setup on most Fieros. (Some early L4's had a 2 speed fan)<P>In a "perfectly good" Fiero cooling system, the coolant can and will continue to rise in temperature until the fan kicks on at 235. Typical temps are 210-220F without the fan running. And that's with free air temperatures of 60-70F. Get into slow traffic or warmer weather and coolant temps will easily rise above that.<P>This is the reason Hypertech requires the low temp switch for their chip & 160 thermostat package. It doesn't do any good to lower the thermostat value if the cooling system still wants to run at 210-220 or more.<P>A switch with 200 on/180 off, or even a little lower range, will work fine for either the 180 or 195 thermostats. With this switch you'll likely run the fan when ever the car is warmed up. This is not a bad thing. It keeps the coolant temp very stable which is really important to consistant engine performance.<P>If you should want the fan turning on and off then you would want to pick a switch that has a shutoff just above the thermostat value. So for a 195 thermostat you'd want a switch that shuts off at about 200, which means it probly won't come on untill 210-215. Even that is better than 235. About the only time I'd use a switch like this would be if I did allot of highway driving. You could endup switching on/off allot otherwise and that's worse for the fan and relay than just running it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
